menu "X-Input Example Configuration"

    config XINPUT_EXAMPLE_COMPOSITE
        bool "Composite: add a WebUSB vendor interface beside the X-Input one"
        default n
        help
            Off: X-Input is the only function, the device carries the Xbox 360
            controller's own identity (0x045E:0x028E, a 0xFF device class) and a
            PC binds it as that controller.

            On: a WebUSB vendor interface is added (as a configuration console
            would be), so the device is a composite one (0x00 device class). It
            then advertises the "XUSB10" compatible ID for the X-Input interface
            through the Microsoft OS 2.0 descriptor, which Windows' Xbox 360
            driver matches, and Linux's xpad matches the interface class under
            the Microsoft vendor ID. Needs the built-in vendor class driver:
            build with `-DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.composite"`.

    config XINPUT_EXAMPLE_COMPOSITE_PID
        hex "Composite product ID (under vendor ID 0x045E)"
        depends on XINPUT_EXAMPLE_COMPOSITE
        default 0x0E60
        help
            Windows binds its Xbox 360 driver to the WHOLE device when the
            device's VID:PID is one its driver INF lists by hardware ID (the
            retail controllers: 0x028E, 0x0719, ...), which would hide the vendor
            interface. A composite device therefore needs a product ID that INF
            does not list; the vendor ID stays Microsoft's so Linux's xpad
            recognizes the interface. Emulation / testing of your own device only.

endmenu
